### ✅ Step 3: Save Customer Object into a List and Serialize

Here's a sample code to save a `Customer` object into a list and serialize it to JSON (which can be sent to a database):

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using System.Text.Json;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // Create a customer
        Customer customer = new Customer
        {
            Username = "john_doe",
            Password = "securepassword123",
            FullName = "John Doe",
            Email = "john.doe@example.com",
            Address = "123 Main St, Cityville"
        };

        // Create a list to store customers
        List<Customer> customers = new List<Customer>
        {
            customer
        };

        // Serialize the list to JSON
        string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(customers, new JsonSerializerOptions { WriteIndented = true });

        // Output the JSON (you can send this to a database)
        Console.WriteLine(json);

        // Optional: Save to a file
        File.WriteAllText("customers.json", json);
    }
}
```

